				{
					"id": "taTsUzbiTkLD4icgE",
					"name": "Thick Hide",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 2, all locations. Stacks with worn armour.\n\nLore: An Ork's skin is thick, rubbery and mottled green, tough enough on its own to turn a knife-edge or a glancing shot that would open a human. Every depiction of an unarmoured Ork brawling — Codex background, Crowley's and Brooks' fiction alike — treats a bit of greenskin hide as meaningfully tougher than bare human flesh, well before any scrap armour goes on top of it.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance 2 (B46, 5/level, all locations, 10 points): modest next to the Ossmodula's partial DR 3 or Custodes-tier toughness, but applies everywhere and is on top of whatever armour an Ork straps on.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 10,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 2
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tcNDSvxL-yq4HhCds",
					"name": "High Pain Threshold",
					"reference": "B59",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Orks shrug off wounds, keep fighting with an arm hanging by a tendon, and treat a lost eye as a minor inconvenience worth a good story later — a running joke across every Ork-focussed novel and a staple of Codex background (\"'e's still comin'!\").\n\nDesign: Basic Set High Pain Threshold (B59), 10 points, unmodified.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "t6-ubLeVPW-CvxM2m",
					"name": "Hard to Kill",
					"reference": "B58",
					"local_notes": "+3 to HT rolls made solely to avoid death.\n\nLore: An Ork that should be dead — gutshot, missing limbs, skull dented in — routinely keeps swinging until the fight is over, and often survives afterwards besides. Ghazghkull Thraka: Prophet of the Waaagh! plays this for both horror and comedy in the same scene more than once.\n\nDesign: Hard to Kill 3 (B58, 2/level, 6 points): +3 to death checks, below a Warboss's own extra levels below.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 2,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 3,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tlRrawanJxbrNhSzT",
					"name": "Rapid Healing",
					"reference": "B79",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Wounds that would fester or kill close over in days rather than weeks — part of the same fungal biology that lets a beaten Ork mob be back at strength within a season. Codex background is explicit that Ork tissue heals unnaturally fast; Black Library plays it as \"give it a week and 'e'll be grand.\"\n\nDesign: Basic Set Rapid Healing (B79, 5 points): doubles effective HT for natural recovery rolls.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 5
				},
				{
					"id": "t1kZTW-Bn0pqOR9AQ",
					"name": "Injury Tolerance (No Brain)",
					"reference": "B61",
					"local_notes": "A hit to the skull or eye is resolved as a hit to the face instead; there is no separate brain to target for extra injury. House rule, not a further purchased trait: an Ork who loses a limb — or, per the fiction, even the head — to a crippling or removal wound is not necessarily dead from that alone. A Painboy (or any suitably brutal battlefield medic) who recovers the part and staples, bolts or wires it back on within a scene or two restores it to function once the patient heals, using Surgery per the framework's skill table. This is a GM-adjudicated narrative rule that Hard to Kill, Rapid Healing and this trait together make plausible, not a separate priced advantage.\n\nLore: Orks are famous for surviving injuries that would kill anything else outright, up to and including having a head sewn back on by a Doks after a losing argument — a beat repeated across Codex background, Brutal Kunnin' and countless tabletop-derived anecdotes (\"'e's had worse\"). Ork brain function is diffuse and fungal rather than centralised in the human sense, which is offered here as the setting explanation rather than a citable page reference.\n\nDesign: Injury Tolerance, No Brain (B61, 5 points): mechanically small on its own, but combined with Hard to Kill and Rapid Healing above it is what makes \"shrugs off a head wound\" true at the table instead of just in the fluff text.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 5
				},
				{
					"id": "tYt21gu9h2T9tmRuN",
					"name": "Resistant to Disease and Toxins",
					"reference": "B81",
					"local_notes": "Resistant (Common: Disease and Toxins), Immunity.\n\nLore: An Ork can eat rotten meat, drink promethium-cut moonshine and wade through toxic industrial runoff without noticeable harm — Codex background treats Ork constitutions as effectively immune to the diseases and poisons that trouble other races, part of the same fungal-symbiote biology that lets a Squig be dinner, transport or weapon depending on how it's grown.\n\nDesign: Resistant (B81), Common: Disease and Toxins, Immunity tier — base 15, x1 for Immunity = 15 points, the same tier and math this project's Oolitic Kidney entry uses for a single unified hazard category, pitched below the Custodes' broader Very Common tier (30) since Orks are shown getting stinking drunk and mildly green-sick on purpose for fun, not as flatly unaffected as a Custodian.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 15
				},
				{
					"id": "txYrAvTpDnsz1PgLX",
					"name": "Fungal Physiology (Spore-Born)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Orks are not born but grown: an Ork reproduces by releasing spores that seed fresh fungal growth wherever conditions allow (a mechanism shared with Gretchin, Squigs and the rest of the Ork ecosystem), has no biological sex, and needs neither a mate nor, ordinarily, a trained physician who isn't another Ork with a saw and a good attitude. This is consistent, well-established Codex background and is played straight rather than for shock across Black Library's Ork fiction — an Ork simply is an ambulatory fungal colony shaped like a green thug.\n\nDesign: 0-point lore flag. Every mechanical payoff of this biology (thick hide, extreme pain tolerance, fast healing, survivability of wounds that would kill outright, immunity to disease and poison) is priced separately above; this entry exists purely to document why an Ork needs no gender, no mate, and no bedside manner from anyone.",
					"tags": [
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "t_zhFTfihUNwA3G9g",
					"name": "Bloodlust",
					"reference": "B125",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Fighting is not a means to an end for an Ork, it is the point of being alive: Orks pick fights they don't need to, press an attack past the point of sense, and are consistently written (Ghazghkull Thraka above all) as unable to simply walk away from a fight worth having.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Bloodlust (B125), self-control 12, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "teKdXADwn_67wBWwx",
					"name": "Impulsiveness",
					"reference": "B143",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Orks act first and, on a good day, think about it afterwards — a running theme in how Ork plans in the fiction go from \"good idea\" to \"already happening\" with nothing in between.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Impulsiveness (B143), self-control 12, -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -10,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "tFZne3APPdkw00wy1",
					"name": "Overconfidence",
					"reference": "B94",
					"local_notes": "Lore: An Ork mob genuinely believes it can win any fight it's in, right up until (and sometimes past) the point it's losing badly — a big part of why Ork strategy in the fiction is so often \"charge and find out.\"\n\nDesign: Basic Set Overconfidence (B94), self-control 12, -5 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": -5,
					"cr": 12
				},
				{
					"id": "toj-C9_0lW_aG2Q0f",
					"name": "Ork Kunnin'",
					"reference": "B89",
					"local_notes": "This project's Talent (B89) for practical battlefield cunning: adds\nits level to Tactics, Traps, Camouflage and Observation, the same way\nthe Psyker Talent (Imperium/Traits/Psykers and the Warp.adq) adds to\nits own listed skills, per that file's house extension of Talent's\nnormal rules. An Ork Boy is not clever in the abstract (IQ 8 is IQ\n8), but a mob that has survived a hundred scraps has a bone-deep,\npractical instinct for where the enemy is weak, where the cover is,\nand when a trap will work — instinct this Talent represents directly\nrather than through raw IQ.\n\nLore: Ork tactics look like chaos from outside and are, by any human military-doctrine standard, chaos — and yet Ork mobs consistently find the gap in a defence, spring an ambush, or route around a strongpoint with an efficiency that surprises Imperial commanders who underestimate them. Codex background and both Crowley's and Brooks' novels play this as real, if inarticulate, battlefield cunning rather than dumb luck.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting four skills sits at the Medium Talent rate (B90, 10 points/level, 3-5 skill bracket), the same rate this project's Psyker Talent uses for the same reason. Base entry costs 0 points at level 0; every template above Gretchin buys at least level 1 (10 points, included in the Ork Physiology package below).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Talent",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"points_per_level": 10,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tooRJFb016NbfNPi3",
					"name": "WAAAGH! Gestalt",
					"local_notes": "Roughly ten or more fellow Orks fighting nearby, in earshot of the\nsame battle-roar, count as a WAAAGH! for this bonus. A Weirdboy\ncaught up in a WAAAGH! also treats his own Warp Empowerment as one\nlevel higher (cap +10) for the purpose of which known powers he may\nsafely cast, per the Weirdboy template's notes — this is the\nmechanical hook for \"Orks believe it, so it works, and it works\nbetter the more of them believe it together.\"\n\nLore: A lone Ork is dangerous; a mob of Orks worked up into a full WAAAGH! is a weaponised belief field — braver, louder, and (per Codex background's long-running joke about Ork technology) more likely to have its crude guns and rickety vehicles actually function, because enough Orks are collectively certain that they will. Weirdboyz in particular are consistently shown drawing directly on this collective psychic roar to power their biggest, most dangerous tricks; a Weirdboy isolated from a WAAAGH! is a much smaller threat.\n\nDesign: A narrow conditional Will/Fright Check bonus, eyeballed against Basic's Fearlessness (2/level flat, B55) but restricted to a specific, common-in-play situation rather than always on, so priced well below a flat level of Fearlessness at 5 points. The Weirdboy-specific Empowerment bump is documented, not separately priced, since it only changes which already-bought powers a Weirdboy may safely reach for.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"base_points": 5,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "conditional_modifier",
							"situation": "to resist Fright Checks and Perils of the Warp, while fighting as part of a large mob of fellow Orks (a WAAAGH!)",
							"amount": 2
						}
					]
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tMEAiZbhHaGk0AcsF",
			"name": "Unusual Background (Latent Psychic Potential)",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Psychic ability is a random, rare mutation of the human genome that lets its bearer perceive and touch the immaterium (the warp). Sources disagree on exact frequency — Imperial administrative texts and Dark Heresy-line background both describe it as \"rare but not vanishingly so,\" on the order of a handful of latent potentials per hive-sized population, with usably strong psykers far rarer still. Treat any precise ratio as flavour, not census data.\n\nDesign: Required to take Psyker (Talent), Warp Empowerment, or any trait carrying the Warp Sorcery power modifier below. Basic Set B261 leaves Unusual Background costs to the GM; 10 points reflects that the ability itself is not learned but grants access to an entire framework of further abilities, roughly the going rate other published settings use for \"you may buy from this power list at all.\"",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"base_points": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "toB5TTbrXkFuZgj4j",
			"name": "Psyker",
			"reference": "TSOR5",
			"local_notes": "This project's Sorcery Talent (Thaumatology: Sorcery, TSOR5) analogue:\napplies as Sorcery Talent applies to a sorcerer's skills in TSOR, just\nto a 40k-specific list — Psyniscience, Meditation, Hidden Lore (the\nWarp), and the Will roll to resist Perils of the Warp (see above) — a\nhouse extension of Talent's normal \"add to listed skills\" rule,\nprecedented by Magery's and Sorcery Talent's own habit of aiding\nrelated rolls beyond pure spellcasting. Reaction bonus: +1/level from\nother sanctioned psykers and the Astra Telepathica.\n\nPsyker (Talent) and Warp Empowerment are separate purchases on separate\naxes, exactly as Sorcery Talent and Sorcerous Empowerment are in TSOR:\nthis trait only sharpens related skills and Perils resistance, while\nWarp Empowerment (above) is what actually gates which powers a psyker\nmay know. A high-Talent, low-Empowerment psyker is a sharp, well-drilled\nmind who simply hasn't learned much yet; the reverse is a raw prodigy\nwho is clumsy with the skills around their gift.\n\nImperial Assignment scale (Adeptus Astra Telepathica / Black Ships),\nweakest to strongest, mapped to Talent level. See Warp Empowerment above\nfor the matching Empowerment level/point-cap column, which is the real\ngate on ability selection:\n  Iota    — Talent 0 (latent; register but rarely take Psyker traits at all)\n  Zeta    — Talent 1\n  Eta     — Talent 1\n  Theta   — Talent 2\n  Epsilon — Talent 2  (typical Wyrdvane conscript)\n  Delta   — Talent 3  (typical Sanctioned Primaris Psyker)\n  Gamma   — Talent 4\n  Beta    — Talent 4  (many Astropaths)\n  Alpha   — Talent 5  (Astartes Librarians, Black Ship candidates)\n  Alpha-Plus — Talent 6+ (beyond-play tier: Primarchs,\n    the most powerful Inquisitors, the worst Chaos sorcerers)\n\nLore: The Iota-to-Alpha-Plus assignment scale is drawn from the Adeptus Astra Telepathica's own grading of psychic potential as depicted across Dark Heresy-line material and repeated in Codex/Imperial Armour background; Black Library uses the same grades in passing (e.g. Eisenhorn-cycle and Ciaphas Cain references to Alpha-class talents). \"Alpha-Plus\" specifically denotes the rarest, most dangerous talents the Black Ships process.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting four things (three skills plus the Perils resistance roll) sits at Basic's \"Medium Talent\" rate of 10 points/level (B90, three-to-five-skill bracket) rather than 5 (one-two skills) or 15 (six-nine skills) — the same rate Sorcery Talent itself uses (TSOR5, 10/level). Capped at level 6 for player characters; higher grades are GM-reference only, per the framework's \"Beyond play\" tier.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Talent",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"points_per_level": 10,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 2,
			"base_points": 0,
			"max_levels": 6
		},
		{
			"id": "tKIKfthrpRhPyb8kL",
			"name": "Warp Empowerment",
			"reference": "TSOR4",
			"local_notes": "A direct reskin of Sorcerous Empowerment (Thaumatology: Sorcery, TSOR4):\nthe psyker's raw, trained capacity to draw on and shape the warp, as\ndistinct from Psyker (Talent, TSOR5's Sorcery Talent analogue, below)\nwhich only sharpens related skills. Requires Unusual Background (Latent\nPsychic Potential). It is the prerequisite for every Warp Sorcery-tagged\nability in this file (see Witch-sight and each discipline below); the\nNavigator's Third Eye and the Pariah/Untouchable genes carry no Warp\nSorcery modifier and so need no Warp Empowerment.\n\nCap, cast per Thaumatology: Sorcery (TSOR): a psyker may only know a\npower whose full, modified point cost is no greater than the point cost\nof their own Warp Empowerment (10 + 10/level). A power bought at a\nhigher level (more dice, more range, etc.) costs proportionally more and\nmay outgrow a psyker's Empowerment even after they first learned it at a\ncheaper level — raising the level, and cost, of Warp Empowerment is how\na psyker \"grows into\" costlier or higher-level workings.\n\nImperial Assignment scale (Adeptus Astra Telepathica / Black Ships)\nmapped to Warp Empowerment level and the point cap that gives, worked\nout from this file's actual (Warp Sorcery -20% + Perils of the Warp)\nmodified costs:\n\n  Grade      | Empowerment level | Cap (10+10/lvl) | What the cap reaches\n  Iota       | 1                  | 20              | cheapest powers only: e.g. Smite (3), Endurance (2), Iron Arm (4), Fire Bolt (4), Assail (4), Mind Probe (14), Foresight (18) — register but rarely learn more than one or two\n  Zeta       | 1                  | 20              | as Iota; low Psyker Talent limits skill use, not what they can know\n  Eta        | 1                  | 20              | as Zeta\n  Theta      | 2                  | 30              | adds Molten Beam (9), Crush (11), Psychic Scream (12), Sanctuary (13), Null Zone (23), Terrify (28)\n  Epsilon    | 2                  | 30              | as Theta (typical Wyrdvane conscript)\n  Delta      | 3                  | 40              | adds Scrier's Gaze (35), Dominate (35), Gate of Infinity (40) — the full non-Great-Power roster (typical Sanctioned Primaris Psyker)\n  Gamma      | 3                  | 40              | as Delta, better skills from a higher Psyker Talent\n  Beta       | 4                  | 50              | full roster, with headroom to push a known leveled power to a higher level/more dice (many Astropaths)\n  Alpha      | 5                  | 60              | full roster, generous headroom for high-level workings (Astartes Librarians, Black Ship candidates)\n  Alpha-Plus | 7+ (GM only)       | 80+             | approaching Great Power territory; Vortex of Doom (280+ as usually bought) is still normally out of reach even here\n\nA character need not buy Warp Empowerment up to their grade's full\nceiling: templates in this project buy only the level their actually-\nknown powers need, and note the grade's theoretical ceiling separately.\n\nLore: The Iota-to-Alpha-Plus assignment scale is drawn from the Adeptus Astra Telepathica's own grading of psychic potential as depicted across Dark Heresy-line material and repeated in Codex/Imperial Armour background; Black Library uses the same grades in passing (e.g. Eisenhorn-cycle and Ciaphas Cain references to Alpha-class talents). \"Alpha-Plus\" specifically denotes the rarest, most dangerous talents the Black Ships process.\n\nDesign: Sorcerous Empowerment (TSOR4) costs 10 base + 10/level (level 1 = 20 points, each further level +10) in the GCS master Thaumatology data; reused verbatim here under a 40k-appropriate name, since the underlying mechanic — \"how much warp-power you can channel at all, gating which spells/powers you may know by their modified cost\" — is exactly what an Imperial Assignment grade represents. The level-to-grade mapping above is derived, not asserted: it walks every power in this file's actual modified cost (recomputed under the new Warp Sorcery -20% modifier, see below) and finds the cheapest Empowerment level whose cap clears it, so \"Iota can only cast minor powers\" and \"Alpha can cast the biggest non-Great-Power ability\" are literally true of the numbers, not just flavour text.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Talent",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"points_per_level": 10,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 5,
			"base_points": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "tnppkCbpbuHIWf5rE",
			"name": "Witch-sight",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The first, mildest sign of the gift: an unlearned, passive prickling sense of daemonic taint, psychic activity, and warp corruption — how unsanctioned psykers are so often found, and how veteran Inquisitors and Space Marines notice a place has gone wrong before anything visible happens. Widely depicted; no single canonical source, consistent across Black Library and Dark Heresy-line material alike.\n\nDesign: Built as Detect (Psychic Activity & Daemonic Taint), an uncommon but not vanishingly rare category, base 10 per Basic's Detect cost table (B47-48). No Perils of the Warp: it is involuntary background sense, not a deliberate act of channelling. Full modified cost: 10 x 0.8 = 8 points; needs only Warp Empowerment level 1 (cap 20) — even an Iota-grade latent can have it, matching how often unsanctioned psykers are found by exactly this unlearned sense.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Psyker",
				"Imperium"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "maMkzvSZ6MWL378BX",
					"name": "Warp Sorcery",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				}
			],
			"base_points": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "t2SHbB4pkhfo1_w9w",
			"name": "Social Stigma (Dangerous 'Ead)",
			"reference": "B155",
			"local_notes": "Second-Class Citizen-equivalent, within Ork society specifically.\n\nLore: Other Orks respect a Weirdboy's power and are visibly nervous around it in the same breath — nobody wants to be standing too close when the \"funny feeling\" starts, and a Weirdboy is watched, humoured, and occasionally \"helped\" by a Painboy's tools rather than fully trusted.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Social Stigma (Second-Class Citizen)-tier cost (B155), -5 points, reused here for the narrower \"everyone's a little afraid of you and it shows\" social cost, distinct from the Imperium's own Sanctioned Psyker Social Stigma since Ork society has no legal apparatus to register or license a Weirdboy in the first place.",
			"tags": [
				"Disadvantage",
				"Social",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"base_points": -5
		},
		{
			"id": "tGXcP4Nn7Uu6kv3P_",
			"name": "'Eadbanger",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A short-ranged psychic bludgeon that a Weirdboy throws at a single target's skull — described across Ork fiction as looking exactly like it sounds, a target's head rocking or worse under an unseen blow.\n\nDesign: Innate Attack (Crushing, 5/die, B61) with the standard Warp Sorcery/ Perils pair, net -30% (same derivation as this project's Assail), reflavoured for Ork psychic force rather than fine telekinetic control. Full modified cost: 5 x 0.7 = 3.5 points/level; needs Warp Empowerment level 1 (cap 20).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "myHeRmVnGqj0Yg54R",
					"name": "Warp Sorcery",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mU6hHhycBZdVCWUNR",
					"name": "Perils of the Warp",
					"cost_adj": "-10%"
				}
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 2
		},
		{
			"id": "taXZvJBxzg97Atgrg",
			"name": "Fists of Gork (an' Mork)",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Channels raw WAAAGH! force into a Weirdboy's own fists (or whoever's fists he's pointing at), letting even a bare-handed blow crack armour plate — the source of the tabletop \"Warpath\"/\"Fists of Gork\" tradition of a Weirdboy briefly turning his mob into something that hits far harder than it should.\n\nDesign: Striking ST (5/level, B) with Warp Sorcery/Perils, net -30% (the same derivation this project's Sanctic Daemonology uses for Hammerhand). Full modified cost: 5 x 0.7 = 3.5 points/level; needs Warp Empowerment level 1 (cap 20).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "mbvRWklX5377kh25A",
					"name": "Warp Sorcery",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mEw9qN3mlt_GthMpK",
					"name": "Perils of the Warp",
					"cost_adj": "-10%"
				}
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 2
		},
		{
			"id": "TJMWSl8pCHG0pB3KP",
			"name": "Warpath",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Warpath is the two halves of the same WAAAGH!-fuelled blessing a Weirdboy throws over his own mob before a charge: sudden strength and sudden speed, together. Tabletop and Codex background both describe Weirdboyz using exactly this kind of squad-wide buff in the moments before Orks hit a line.\n\nDesign: Grouped as GCS sums the children: 12 (Heroic Feats of Strength) + 6 (Warp Speed, Mob, at level 1) = 18 points at its default starting level.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t3D_MkLDAd1xBWW6f",
					"name": "Heroic Feats of Strength",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A howled WAAAGH! that fills a Weirdboy's whole mob with sudden, reckless strength for the charge.\n\nDesign: Basic's Heroic Feats of Strength (flat 10) with Area Effect (+50%) plus Warp Sorcery/Perils, net +20%. Full modified cost: 10 x 1.2 = 12 points; needs Warp Empowerment level 2 (cap 30) — the same build this project's Librarius: Might of Heroes uses.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mlGDxalDJIm4yxrOr",
							"name": "Area Effect",
							"cost_adj": "+50%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mNvRA4GeSsrOuoZOZ",
							"name": "Warp Sorcery",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						},
						{
							"id": "m3HqSdsGoYmd5Ve4s",
							"name": "Perils of the Warp",
							"cost_adj": "-10%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tGkvINAkbj9LVcJ1z",
					"name": "Warp Speed (Mob)",
					"local_notes": "Lore: The same burst quickens the whole mob's feet, turning a shambling charge into a genuine sprint.\n\nDesign: Increased Basic Speed (5 points per +0.25) with Area Effect (+50%) plus Warp Sorcery/Perils, net +20%. Full modified cost: 5 x 1.2 = 6 points/level; needs Warp Empowerment level 2 (cap 30).",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Orks"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "m_vCYEsRME3pcTQh9",
							"name": "Area Effect",
							"cost_adj": "+50%"
						},
						{
							"id": "m7tAOn-b-zZZtt6_e",
							"name": "Warp Sorcery",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mI9AeZQlMKKxMf_EU",
							"name": "Perils of the Warp",
							"cost_adj": "-10%"
						}
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "tK91BhmqN308NCehg",
			"name": "Da Jump",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Weirdboy's signature and most feared trick: he grabs the nearest mob of Boyz, screams, and the whole lot vanish and reappear somewhere else on the battlefield an instant later — sometimes exactly where intended, sometimes scattered, occasionally (per the fiction's favourite running joke about Da Jump) several yards in the air. Nate Crowley's Ghazghkull Thraka: Prophet of the Waaagh! uses Da Jump for both a genuine tactical surprise and outright slapstick in the same book.\n\nDesign: Built on Basic's own Warp advantage (B99, instant travel to any place you have seen/visualised, flat 100), matching this project's Gate of Infinity build, with Area Effect (+50%, B102, brings the whole mob) added on top, Preparation Required (-20%, a moment's howling concentration), Uncontrollable (-30%, B113, a Weirdboy can't always land the mob exactly where he meant to — the game's own \"scatter\" mechanic, modelled honestly rather than smoothed over), Warp Sorcery (-20%) and the Severe Perils tier (-20%, a botched Jump is exactly the kind of interstellar-workings-adjacent disaster that tier exists for). Net: +50-20-30-20-20 = -40%. Full modified cost: 100 x 0.6 = 60 points — needing Warp Empowerment level 5 (cap 60), right at the cap with no headroom, marking Da Jump as the single hardest power for an ordinary Weirdboy to reach, exactly matching its status in the fiction as the biggest trick in a Weirdboy's book.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Orks"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "mAv1pQjeGcU1q65NZ",
					"name": "Area Effect",
					"cost_adj": "+50%"
				},
				{
					"id": "m_c_k1oEk4VFxXSFT",
					"name": "Preparation Required",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mxWAJIFY_Y3PMJ2Z1",
					"name": "Uncontrollable",
					"cost_adj": "-30%"
				},
				{
					"id": "mYyJGd_mP1Tajyl7s",
					"name": "Warp Sorcery",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				},
				{
					"id": "m13K9Wr7w1vXl0VIr",
					"name": "Perils of the Warp (Severe)",
					"cost_adj": "-20%"
				}
			],
			"base_points": 100
		}
	],
